(The equivalent warning is main.c:47)

So Q is a &quot;tuple-struct&quot;. In rust you can define a struct like<p>struct Q&lt;T&gt;  {
  value: Option&lt;T&gt;
}<p>with a named field or you can define it like<p>struct&lt;T&gt; Q(Some&lt;T&gt;)<p>which is the same struct layout but without an explicit name for the value. In that case, the field is implicitly names `0`<p>So q.0 = Some(foo) is equivalent to q.value = Some(foo).<p>The Some(..) is just one variant of the Option enum:<p>enum Option&lt;T&gt; {
  None,
  Some(T)
}<p>Option represents a value that may or may not be initialized. For uninitialized values you use the None variant and then you can initialize it to Some(value).<p>The C rough equivalent would be<p>struct Q {
  void* value;
}<p>struct Q q = q { NULL };
q-&gt;value = ...

&quot;Some(...)&quot; means that this Option&lt;T&gt; does have a value of type T in it; &quot;None&quot; means it doesn&#x27;t.<p>&quot;q.0&quot; is not really needed, it&#x27;s a struct with unnamed fields. Here is the same with named fields:<p><pre><code>  struct Q&lt;T: &#x27;static&gt; { pub x: Option&lt;T&gt; }
  ...
  let l = String::new();
  let mut q = Q { x: None };
  q.x = Some(&amp;l);</code></pre>

